[expect] "I am going to riff on a new idea for a chapter. This is on the devil's interval. I was interested in the history of barbershop music. Some AI was explaining how it started in the 1910s but became truly popular in the 30s when a certain group released barbershop music. It strikes me that one reason that was when it became popular was suddenly Victrola's were popular and people could buy and listen to music and didn't have to wait for it to be locally available through vocal groups.  But the AI went on to tell me that it really started with black music groups hanging on their own and communities where they didn't have access to white people's concerts so they made their own music. The AI went on to offer me the secret of barbershop music, which turns out to be the barbershop seventh. That's not a no in the western scale it's a bent version of the seventh tuned way lower than a major seventh somewhere between a major and a minor. I know something about tuning, and recognize that to be a very consonant whole number ratio something, like 7 4:which, would be a beautiful sound that's not available to the Western scale which, has no multiple multiples of seven in it. Even the just-tempered scale doesn’t have this note, let alone the tempered scale. The AI told me that note that tuning was independently discovered by both barbershop music and jazz music I said wait a minute those both come from black roots and I bet the note came out out of Africa along with the slaves. The AI told me that that's exactly where it came from and gave me examples of traditional African music known to use that interval. Jazz music also bends the fourth and what's called the "devil's" interval. I always had the impression that the term came from the music theory of the Middle Ages. But now I wonder if the term is much more recent and comes from slave music. Certainly, the slaves brought with them practices and beliefs that were not "Christian," the most infamous being voodoo, and it would make sense to me that the musical theories of the time (this would be in the 18th century, I guess) we would pick up on, if not invent the term "devil's interval" to describe this aspect of their music. Sometimes we label things as evil because they are unfamiliar and don’t fit into our preconceptions of how the world works. And yet the same adventurous, exotic foreign sensations. Can add spice to our lives and can potentially open up a world more beautiful than the one we know .  I tend to think of the equal temperament scale as a kind of cartoon version of natural tunings. We learned growing up that that's how music is supposed to sound. When we hear a choir tuning its intervals, it sounds magical to us. So bending a little bit and lead to both heaven and hell"
[send] "I am going to riff on a new idea for a chapter. This is on the devil's interval. I was interested in the history of barbershop music. Some AI was explaining how it started in the 1910s but became truly popular in the 30s when a certain group released barbershop music. It strikes me that one reason that was when it became popular was suddenly Victrola's were popular and people could buy and listen to music and didn't have to wait for it to be locally available through vocal groups.  But the AI went on to tell me that it really started with black music groups hanging on their own and communities where they didn't have access to white people's concerts so they made their own music. The AI went on to offer me the secret of barbershop music, which turns out to be the barbershop seventh. That's not a no in the western scale it's a bent version of the seventh tuned way lower than a major seventh somewhere between a major and a minor. I know something about tuning, and recognize that to be a very consonant whole number ratio something, like 7 4:which, would be a beautiful sound that's not available to the Western scale which, has no multiple multiples of seven in it. Even the just-tempered scale doesn’t have this note, let alone the tempered scale. The AI told me that note that tuning was independently discovered by both barbershop music and jazz music I said wait a minute those both come from black roots and I bet the note came out out of Africa along with the slaves. The AI told me that that's exactly where it came from and gave me examples of traditional African music known to use that interval. Jazz music also bends the fourth and what's called the "devil's" interval. I always had the impression that the term came from the music theory of the Middle Ages. But now I wonder if the term is much more recent and comes from slave music. Certainly, the slaves brought with them practices and beliefs that were not "Christian," the most infamous being voodoo, and it would make sense to me that the musical theories of the time (this would be in the 18th century, I guess) we would pick up on, if not invent the term "devil's interval" to describe this aspect of their music. Sometimes we label things as evil because they are unfamiliar and don’t fit into our preconceptions of how the world works. And yet the same adventurous, exotic foreign sensations. Can add spice to our lives and can potentially open up a world more beautiful than the one we know .  I tend to think of the equal temperament scale as a kind of cartoon version of natural tunings. We learned growing up that that's how music is supposed to sound. When we hear a choir tuning its intervals, it sounds magical to us. So bending a little bit and lead to both heaven and hell"

